    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm a bit worried about the preservation aspect too, though. When we're dealing with these older items, things can get pretty chaotic if the classification doesn't account for the nuances of how the records were originally kept. If the metadata isn't precise, it feels like things just fall into a sort of \"black hole\" of disorder where you can't find anything. How do these two systems handle cases where the original organizational logic is totally messy or non-existent?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When encountering chaotic original organizational logic, both systems rely heavily on the cataloging rules applied *during* accessioning rather than perfectly reflecting pre-existing chaos. LCC generally handles large gaps or unstructured materials by allowing more room for descriptive, non-subject-based metadata, often using unique local call numbers augmented by LCC elements. DDC, being more prescriptive, can force materials into broader subject buckets, potentially masking valuable original contextual information. For messy, complex historical items, supplementary descriptive metadata fields—independent of the primary classification—are crucial for both systems.",
      "source": "target",
