    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So it's more about imposing a new order over the old one. My main concern is that if we don't get that local metadata right, we're going to end up with a collection that essentially \"hides\" itself. It's like items just vanish into the piles because the classification is too blunt. Do you think there's a risk of certain rare items being practically lost to researchers because they've been misfiled or buried under broad categories?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, there is a significant risk that overly broad classification or poorly executed local metadata can lead to \"hiding\" rare items, effectively making them functionally lost to researchers. If the classification is too blunt, a highly specialized manuscript might be placed under a general \"Literature\" heading instead of a precise archival or regional category. Therefore, prioritizing detailed, descriptive metadata—such as provenance, date range, and original collection names—is the best defense against intellectual loss, regardless of whether you choose DDC or LCC. This level of contextual data acts as a lifeline when the standard classification fails.",
      "source": "target",
